"# Serialization Graph Construction\n\nTwo graphs are relevant, matching the two readings established earlier: **SG-1** for the history *as written* (classical conflict serializability), and **SG-2** the **MVCC serialization graph** under snapshot isolation with concurrent T1/T2. I give nodes, edges, adjacency, and cycle analysis for each.\n\n---\n\n## Graph SG-1 — Classical Conflict Graph (history as written, order T1 → T2 → T3)\n\n### Nodes\n`{T1, T2, T3}` — committed transactions only (T0 omitted; all reads here see T0's versions, no T0 node needed in the classical graph).\n\n### Edges (labeled by conflict type and item)\n\n| Edge | Justification (operation pair) |\n|---|---|\n| T1 → T2 | wr(A): T1.W(A) → T2.R(A) |\n| T1 → T2 | wr(B): T1.W(B) → T2.R(B) |\n| T1 → T2 | ww(A): T1.W(A) → T2.W(A) |\n| T1 → T2 | ww(B): T1.W(B) → T2.W(B) |\n| T2 → T3 | wr(A): T2.W(A) → T3.R(A) |\n| T2 → T3 | wr(B): T2.W(B) → T3.R(B) |\n\n(No edge T1 → T3 directly: every T1→T3 conflict is dominated by the path through T2; classical SG uses direct conflicts only.)\n\n### Adjacency list\n```\nT1 → { T2 }\nT2 → { T3 }\nT3 → { }\n```\n\n### Acyclicity\nTopological order exists: **T1, T2, T3**. No back edges, no cycles.\n\n✅ **SG-1 is acyclic** — and the topological order *is* the history itself: it is already a serial execution, hence trivially conflict-serializable (equivalent serial order `T1 → T2 → T3`).\n\n---\n\n## Graph SG-2 — MVCC Serialization Graph under Snapshot Isolation (T1, T2 concurrent)\n\nVersion orders: `A0(T0) < A1(T1) < A2(T2)` and `B0(T0) < B1(T1) < B2(T2)`. T1 and T2 take the T0 snapshot; T3's snapshot assumed = T0. **Worst case: both T1 and T2 commit.**\n\n### Nodes\n`{T0, T1, T2, T3}` (T0 = last pre-history committer, source of all snapshots).\n\n### Edges (MVCC rules)\n\n| Edge | Type | Justification |\n|---|---|---|\n| T0 → T1 | wr / reads-from | T1 reads A0, B0 |\n| T0 → T2 | wr / reads-from | T2 reads A0, B0 |\n| T0 → T3 | wr / reads-from | T3 reads A0, B0 |\n| T1 → T2 | ww (A), ww (B) | version order A1 < A2, B1 < B2 |\n| T2 → T1 | rw (A), rw (B) | T2 read A0/B0; **next version** in version order (A1/B1) installed by T1 |\n| T3 → T1 | rw (A), rw (B) | T3 read A0/B0; next version installed by T1 |\n\n*(Anti-dependency edges point to the writer of the immediately-next version only — hence T3 → T1, not T3 → T2.)*\n\n### Adjacency list\n```\nT0 → { T1, T2, T3 }\nT1 → { T2 }\nT2 → { T1 }        ← back edge\nT3 → { T1 }\n```\n\n### Cycle analysis\nDFS from T0 reaches T1 → T2 → **T1** (back edge). Detected cycle:\n\n```\nT1 ──ww(A,B)──▶ T2 ──rw(A,B)──▶ T1\n```\n\n❌ **SG-2 (both-commit) is NOT acyclic** — a 2-cycle between T1 and T2 ⇒ non-serializable; the anomaly it encodes is the **lost update** on A and B.\n\n### Committed projection (snapshot isolation applies first-committer-wins)\n\nT2 aborts; all T2 edges (`T1→T2` ww ×2, `T2→T1` rw ×2, `T0→T2`) are removed:\n\n```\nT0 → { T1, T3 }\nT1 → { }\nT3 → { T1 }\n```\n\n✅ **Acyclic.** Topological order `T0 → T3 → T1` gives the equivalent serial order **T3 → T1**. (Symmetrically, if T1 had been the aborter: `T0 → {T2, T3}, T3 → {T2}` — also acyclic, serial order T3 → T2.)\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n| Graph | Nodes | Edge count | Cycle? | Serializable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SG-1 (history as written) | T1, T2, T3 | 6 | No | ✅ Yes (serial order T1→T2→T3) |\n| SG-2 full (T1 & T2 both commit) | T0, T1, T2, T3 | 7 | **Yes: T1 ⇄ T2** | ❌ No (lost update) |\n| SG-2 committed projection (FCW) | T0, T1, T3 | 3 | No | ✅ Yes (serial order T3→T1) |\n\nThe decisive structure is the **2-cycle `T1 →ww→ T2 →rw→ T1`**: snapshot isolation's first-committer-wins rule breaks it by aborting the second writer, restoring acyclicity in the committed graph."